    kind: work_item  # 4f61
    title: "194 I/O-boundary chains anchor io_edge_src to a FILE node, not the enclosing callable"  # 4f61
    status: todo_hard  # 4f61
    priority: 2  # 4f61
    parent:  # 4f61
    tags:  # 4f61
    - dogfood  # 4f61
    isbefore: []  # 4f61
    duplicate_of: []  # 4f61
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 4f61
    pr_ref:  # 4f61
    description: "- **Subject:** A subset of I/O-boundary chains are anchored to a file node rather than to the callable performing the I/O.\n- **Mechanic:** For these chains the `io_edge_src` resolves to a FILE node (not a callable), so the chain records which file the I/O occurs in but not which function performs it. The producer does not resolve `io_edge_src` to the enclosing callable for these cases.\n- **Expectation:** `io_edge_src` should resolve to the enclosing callable so a consumer can identify the function performing the I/O.\n- **Scope:** 194 of 7949 I/O-boundary chains are anchored to a FILE node as `io_edge_src` (185 of them in the external-potential bucket).\n- **Exposure:** A consumer reading I/O-boundary chains to attribute I/O to specific functions (e.g. for an impact or security review).\n- **Consequence:** For those 194 chains the consumer cannot tell which function performs the I/O — only the containing file — so function-level I/O attribution is impossible for them.\n\n(Filed from dogfooding.)"  # 4f61
    fields: {}  # 4f61
- op: discuss  # b7e4
  at: '2026-08-10T21:13:42Z'  # b7e4
  by: agent  # b7e4
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b7e4
  clock: 2  # b7e4
  nonce: b7e4  # b7e4
  message: "MINIMAL REPRODUCER, 2026-08-10. This item cites 194 chains anchored to a FILE\nnode; here is a five-function fixture that shows the split cleanly, with a\ncorrect sibling on an adjacent line as the control.\n\n    import subprocess, urllib.request\n    def spawn_dotted(cmd): return subprocess.Popen(cmd)\n    def spawn_bare(cmd):\n        from subprocess import Popen\n        return Popen(cmd)\n    def run_baseline(cmd):    return subprocess.run(cmd)\n    def request_control(url): return urllib.request.Request(url)\n\n  primitive                io_edge_src                              entry_points\n  subprocess.run           python:app.py:15-16:run_baseline:function   [run_baseline]\n  urllib.request.Request   python:app.py:19-20:request_control:function [request_control]\n  subprocess.Popen         python:app.py:1-1:file:file                  []          <-- FILE\n  tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile   (no chain in this fixture)\n\nTWO CALL SITES, ONE CHAIN. spawn_dotted and spawn_bare both call Popen and the\nreport carries a SINGLE Popen chain, file-anchored, with no entry point -- so the\ntwo callers are not merely mislabelled, they are unrecoverable from the output.\nsubprocess.run one line away resolves correctly, which is what makes this a\nper-primitive defect rather than a per-file one.\n\nWHY THE DISCRIMINATOR MATTERS BEYOND THIS ITEM. A consumer that asks \"which\nfunctions perform subprocess I/O?\" gets run_baseline and not spawn_*. For\nsubprocess -- the boundary most likely to matter in a safety claim -- that is a\nsilent hole in the answer rather than a visible gap.\n\nALSO OBSERVED, and NOT diagnosed: on the generated catalogue-reach fixture\n(scripts/measure-catalogue-reach.py python) EVERY chain carries entry_points=[]\n-- all 476 of them -- while their io_edge_src NAME slot still identifies the\ncalling function correctly. So `entry_points` being empty is NOT reliable\nevidence of this defect on its own, and a fix that only repopulates entry_points\nwould not address the file-anchored src. The five primitives anchored to a\nnon-caller name there are subprocess.Popen, fileinput.FileInput and the three\ntempfile factories -- all PascalCase-named `function`-kind entries, though\nurllib.request.Request is also PascalCase and anchors correctly, so PascalCase\nalone is not the rule. Mechanism NOT read; do not treat as diagnosed."  # b7e4
- op: discuss  # b510
  at: '2026-09-03T18:22:17Z'  # b510
  by: agent  # b510
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b510
  clock: 3  # b510
  nonce: b510  # b510
  message: "Related, from INV-nopoh (2026-09-03): Go call edges under a package-level\n`var` (every cobra Run handler) now anchor on the VARIABLE's symbol, but the\nmodule_attr_refs for the SAME body (`os.Stderr` read inside the handler) still\nanchor on the FILE, because `_innermost_callable_at` admits function/method\nspans only. So an ipc/logging chain inside a cobra handler has a variable-kind\nsrc on the call and a file-kind src on the attribute read -- the two ends do\nnot meet, the INV-fafol shape this item counts. A fix that admits a package-level\nvariable whose span contains the read (or the alias-edge design in the WI filed\nfor var aliases) closes both. No change made here."  # b510
